{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/gs/state.md at exact fe2d12d6240a63be42518b585c694f508635fb16 on request/submit-deadline-override, under promise git:sha1…a7ab3ab on request git:sha1…d3742a6\n\nConditions met at this exact head.\n\n1. Every command that files a durable act accepts an explicit submit deadline.\nThe twelve that append — state, promise, artifact, review, review-request,\nratify, supersede, reassign-if-unclaimed, batch, merge, land and publish — take\n--deadline; the docset surface gate reads the flags out of the source, so that\nlist is the implementation's own answer rather than mine. GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE\nsupplies the value when the flag is absent, the flag wins where both are given,\nand with neither the deadline is the same ten seconds every caller had before.\n\n2. One place decides it. residentclient.ResolveSubmitDeadline is that place, and\nsubmitRequest, the GitHub connector's submit and the MCP adapter's call deadline\nall take their answer from it; no call site carries its own literal any more. A\nvalue that is not a positive duration is refused, naming what it would accept\n(\"write a positive duration, such as 30s or 2m\"), rather than being replaced by\nthe default the author was trying to escape.\n\nThe adapter needed two further changes to make that honest. Its transport\nbackstop was a fixed forty seconds, which would have cut any call deadline\nraised past it; it is now derived from the longest deadline in the policy, so\nraising the deadline is honoured rather than silently clipped. And the startup\nwiring moved out of main() into configureResidentDeadlines, because a line no\ntest can call is a line nobody has checked.\n\n3. gs batch passes the same deadline to each of its per-act submissions, and its\nrefusal on a timed-out act says what recovery needs: the act may already have\nlanded, and the key to replay under is named. An act with no idempotency_key\ncannot be replayed at all — gs state mints a random key, so a second run appends\na second copy — so that refusal says to find the act first instead. A refused act\nkeeps its own words: telling an author their act may have landed when the\nresident said it did not would be worse than saying nothing.\n\n4. Tests pin each of those. The default is ten seconds; the flag and the variable\neach raise it and the flag wins; unparsable, zero and negative values are all\nrefused; a stalled resident is left to show that the deadline reaches the dial\nrather than merely the context, at two different values; a batch act whose\ndeadline expires reports the key to replay under; and the pinned MCP deadline\ntest now reads the shared source instead of a second literal of its own.\n\n5. Twelve mutations of the new guards, each turning a named test red:\n\n  explicit value beats the environment: TestResolveSubmitDeadlinePrefersExplicitThenEnvironment\n  a value that cannot be read is refused: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  a deadline must be positive: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  an expired deadline is not a refusal: TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal\n  the flag reaches the invocation: TestSubmitDeadlineComesFromTheFlagThenTheEnvironment\n  a refused deadline stops the command: TestSubmittingCommandsRefuseAnUnreadableDeadline\n  the submission dials under this invocation's deadline: TestSubmitRequestWaitsForThisInvocationsDeadline\n  the batch says which act may have landed: TestBatchReportsTheReplayKeyWhenAnActsDeadlineExpires\n  an act with no key is not called replayable: TestBatchSubmitRefusalNamesTheKeyToReplayUnder\n  the adapter reads the environment at startup: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  a refused adapter value does not become the default: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  the backstop outlasts the longest deadline: TestResidentRequestDeadlinesPreserveCallerCancellation\n\nTwo mutations were first recorded as kills and are not. Both were mutants that\ndid not compile, which prove nothing about behaviour, and the harness now reports\nthat separately from a red test. Re-run as compiling mutants, each exposed a real\ndefect, and both are repaired above rather than argued away: TimedOut's explicit\ncontext.DeadlineExceeded and os.ErrDeadlineExceeded branch changed no answer when\ndeleted, because both are net.Error with Timeout reporting true, so it was a\nsecond spelling of the same question and is gone; and the adapter's startup\nwiring survived its mutation because it sat in main(), where no test could reach\nit, which is why it was extracted.\n\n6. docs/reference/gs/ records the flag on all twelve pages that take it, with the\ndefault and the variable in the same row. gs state carries the fuller account —\nwhen to raise it, and why an expired deadline is not a refusal — and gs batch\nsays that batching does not shorten any act's wait and what its refusal will name.\ndocs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md records the variable for the adapter, which has\nno flags.\n\n7. go vet ./... clean and go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./... green at this exact\nhead, exit 0 read from the process rather than through a pipeline, 37 packages,\nno failures.\n\nArchitecture: no layer contract moves. residentclient is the client-side boundary\nfor the local resident and already owned the same shape for actor identity\n(ActorEnvironment, ResolveActor); the deadline joins it as a second setting\nresolved the same way. Nothing changes what the kernel admits, what the fold\ndecides, or what any projection says, and docs/reference/architecture.md is\ntherefore not in this artifact set.","body":{"commit":"fe2d12d6240a63be42518b585c694f508635fb16","path":"docs/reference/gs/state.md"}}